## 1. Scope of the change

File: `packages/compiler/reflaxe/swift/swiftcompiler/SwiftExpr.hx` (only file
touched; `PATCH.diff` is the complete delta).

1. **`switchBindingLines`** (was :5514–5517; the call at :5516): the binding
   route now passes the declared binding type as the destination —
   `switchExpression(sw, v.t)`. The local being bound carries the destination
   requirement.
2. **`switchExpression`** (declaration was :5554 — the review's `:5559` is
   5 lines off; the `:5514-5517` / `:5516` references are correct) gains
   `destination:Null<Type> = null`. Both in-scope derivation sites
   (`switchReturn(sw, 1, false, sw.t)` and the closure header
   `types.of(sw.t)`) now use `dest`, where `dest = usable ? destination :
   sw.t`. `sw.t` remains the fallback **only** for expression positions that
   carry no contract (the `expr` dispatch at :1857 is unchanged and calls it
   without one). Contracts the backend cannot render — `TMono` / `TDynamic`
   after `Context.follow`, which would make `types.of` call `Context.error` —
   are rejected as unusable and fall back, preserving the old behaviour for
   unresolvable types instead of crashing generation.
3. **New `switchArgText(a, pt)`**: a switch used directly as a call argument
   lowers its closure against the callee parameter `pt` (the argument route's
   owning contract). Only `TParenthesis`/`TMeta` wrappers are unwrapped before
   the `TSwitch` check; a `TCast`-wrapped switch deliberately keeps its
   `castText` rendering. Every non-switch argument renders exactly as before
   (the generic `argTexts` branch and the inout-temp branch both route through
   it; the `!`-suffix and the `intToFloatText` post-processing are applied to
   the returned text, unchanged).

Caller audit (complete): `switchExpression` is called from (i)
`switchBindingLines` (now with `v.t`), and (ii) the generic `expr`/`TSwitch`
dispatch at :1857 (fallback, unchanged). `switchBindingLines` is called only
from the `stmtLines` `TVar(v, init) if (init != null && isSwitch(init))`
route. No other in-scope reconstruction of the switch's result type or arm
destination exists on these two routes.

