## 8. Instrumentation blockers (why in-macro logging was infeasible)

All verified against this reflaxe toolchain, not assumed:

- `sys.io.File` is `extern` in the reflaxe std — the constructor cannot be
  called from macro code (`Class<sys.io.File> cannot be called`).
- `haxe.macro.Context` has no `warn` field; `warning`/`info` exist but their
  output is suppressed in the generation phase of this framework (runs
  complete with no diagnostic output).
- `haxe.macro.Position` is an opaque coreType in macro context — no
  `file`/`line`/`source`/`fileName` fields readable for per-site labels.
- `Context.onAfterGenerate` callbacks: neither `Context.error(...)` nor
  `throw` from the callback surfaces (both runs rc=0); `Context.error`
  mid-compilation is the framework's "Build failure" reporting line, not a
  usable channel, and an unconditional `throw` from within
  `switchExpression` aborts the whole generation (rc=1) — usable only as a
  one-shot probe, not a sweep.
- `swiftc` here is a shim; `swiftc -o` does produce a real (ELF) executable
  that runs, but output written through the fuse mount carries no exec bit,
  so binaries had to be copied to local `/tmp` and `chmod +x` before running.

The output-comment hook (the `SEVTRACE` lines) sidesteps all of these: the
generated Swift files are the log, and they are captured as evidence.

